WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.320
Let’s say you really need
to find reliable information

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.600
about the best diet for
high blood pressure,

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.680
or heart disease,
or diabetes.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.120
Where do you go?

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.600
Do you go to a website
sponsored by Big Pharma

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.480
that wants to sell you
pills to fix your problem?

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.520
Or, do you want
to treat the cause?

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.960
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.480
with the latest peer-
reviewed research

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.160
on the best ways to eat
healthy and live longer.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.360
It’s time for the Nutrition
Facts Grab Bag,

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.640
where we look at
the latest science

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:36.240
on a whole variety of topics.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.680
In our first story, we find
out if the health benefits

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.200
of peanut better
include longevity.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.680
According to the largest study
of risk factors for death

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.440
in human history, a poor diet
causes more deaths than anything.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.600
Cigarettes only kill about
eight million people a year,

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.880
whereas humanity’s diet
kills millions more.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.600
What are the worst
aspects of our diet?

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.080
Processed meat? Twinkies? Soda?

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.640
No, the five deadliest
things about our diet are:

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.200
inadequate fruit intake—
not enough fruit;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.440
not enough whole grains;
not enough vegetables;

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.080
too much salt; and not
enough nuts and seeds.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.240
Nuts should come as no surprise
since interventional trials

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.800
have shown that eating nuts
improves artery function,

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.960
and arterial diseases
like heart disease are among

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.720
our leading killers. But
that’s not all nuts can do.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.800
They may also improve
blood sugar control,

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.400
lower cholesterol, suppress inflammation,

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.200
reduce oxidative stress, and
feed our friendly gut flora.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.560
All nuts or just tree nuts?

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.520
What about peanuts?
What about peanut butter?

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.680
About 50 percent of peanut
consumption in the U.S.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.680
is through peanut butter,
but the association between

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.520
peanut butter and mortality
has not been

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:50.320
thoroughly evaluated. To get that
granular, though, we can call on

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.480
the NIH-AARP study, the
largest prospective health

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.200
and diet study in history
that followed more than

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.160
a half million people
since the 1990s.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.360
And…nut consumption in
general appeared to protect

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.240
against all-cause mortality,
meaning nut-eaters live,

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.200
on average, longer lives, and
specifically, are less likely

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.800
to die from cancer, cardiovascular
disease, respiratory disease,

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.000
infectious causes (so, maybe
they help immunity as well),

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.240
liver disease, and kidney disease.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.240
However, no such associations
were found for peanut butter.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:23.840
So, when it comes to living longer,

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.280
peanut butter doesn’t
seem to count. Why?

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.040
Well, we know peanut butter
consumers tend to eat

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.640
more meat, smoke cigarettes,
and were less likely to exercise,

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.720
but the researchers
controlled for all that:

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.160
red meat, white meat, tobacco
use, exercise, and vegetables,

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.000
fruits, and whole grains;
so, it’s not like

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.240
the peanut butter eaters
were just eating more

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:45.840
white bread sandwiches or something.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.960
The researchers didn’t
control for sugar though,

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.760
so it’s possible they could have
been eating more sugary jelly.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.600
It could also be the
processing that goes into making

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.760
peanut butter—the added
transfat, oil, salt, and sugar—

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.000
but regular nuts
are also often eaten

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.240
with added oil, sugar, and salt.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.400
Could it just be the
peanuts themselves?

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.800
Technically, they aren’t
nuts, so maybe they just

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.240
don’t have the same benefits.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.640
But no, a meta-analysis
of all such studies found

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.840
the same nut-like benefits
for whole peanuts,

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.800
but just not peanut butter.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:21.280
Well, one thing missing
from even no salt, oil-free,

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:26.240
sugar-free nut and seed butters
is intact cellular structure.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.520
As I noted in How Not to Diet,
no matter how well we chew

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.800
whole or chopped nuts, some
of the nutrients remain trapped

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.200
in tiny particles that
deliver a bounty of

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.840
prebiotic goodness to
our friendly flora.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:41.120
That makes me wonder if there
would have been any difference

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.280
between chunky and
smooth peanut butter.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.720
In the meanwhile there is
compelling evidence to recommend

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.160
the use of nuts (preferably raw
nuts over salted or toasted)

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.800
and whole or chopped nuts
rather than nut butters,

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.360
at least three times a
week in order to maximize

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:58.160
your chances of living
longer and healthier lives.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.160
In our next story, we look
at what physicians can do

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:03.920
to promote healthy,
life-extending,

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.440
lifestyle changes.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:08.880
A pivotal paper published in
Europe more than a decade ago

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.320
entitled “Healthy Living Is
the Best Revenge” found that

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:15.600
adhering to just four simple
healthy lifestyle factors

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.560
compared to none could
potentially have a strong impact

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.960
on the prevention
of chronic diseases;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:24.160
we’re talking nearly 80 percent
less chronic disease risk,

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.640
slashing diabetes
risk by 93 percent,

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.040
dropping heart attack
risk by 81 percent,

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.560
and cutting stroke risk in half,
and cancer by 36 percent.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.400
Think about what that means.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.560
The potential for preventing
disease and death is enormous.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.800
In the U.S. alone every year,
there are half a million

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.240
heart attacks, half a million
strokes, a million new cases

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.280
of diabetes, and a
million new cancer diagnoses.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.680
The message is clear:
adopting a few healthy behaviors

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:53.440
can have a major impact.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.720
What are those four fabled factors?

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.720
Never smoking, not being obese,
averaging about a half hour of

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:04.960
exercise a day, and adhering to
healthy dietary principles,

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:07.920
like lots of fruits, vegetables,
and whole grains, and less meat.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.440
Follow those four
simple rules and boom!

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:16.160
Enjoy nearly 80 percent reduced
risk of major chronic diseases.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:18.400
What does that mean
for mortality risk?

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:20.800
A similar batch of four healthy

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:22.240
behaviors combined predicted

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:25.040
a four-fold difference
in total mortality,

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:28.480
with an estimated impact
equivalent to 14 years

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.760
in chronological age, meaning
the individuals were dying at

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:36.080
such a reduced rate that it was
as if they were 14 years younger.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:40.800
“Finally, a Regimen to
Extend Human Life Expectancy.”

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:44.080
This commentary was in
reference to this study,

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:46.560
where a similar analysis
of the impact of healthy

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.040
lifestyle behaviors on
life expectancies was made,

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.840
but this time it looked
directly at the U.S. population,

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:55.360
which is particularly important,
since Americans have a

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:57.680
shorter life expectancy
compared with residents

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:00.640
of nearly all other
high-income countries.

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:03.360
The researchers concluded that
adopting a healthy lifestyle

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:06.240
could substantially
reduce premature mortality

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:08.960
and prolong life
expectancy in U.S. adults.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:12.320
Okay, but by how much?

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:15.440
They estimated that adherence
to a low-risk lifestyle

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:19.840
could prolong life expectancy
at age 50 by 14 years

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:22.960
in women and 12.2 years in men.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.480
So, if you’re 50 right now,
instead of only living to 79

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:29.200
if you’re a woman, and
75½ if you’re a man,

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.680
taking even just basic care
of yourself could propel you

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.240
to an average life expectancy
of 93 if you’re a woman

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:38.160
and 87½ if you’re a man.

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:43.760
The bottom line is it’s never
too late to turn back the clock.

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:47.120
A midlife switch just
to the basics, like at least

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.200
five servings fruits
and vegetables a day,

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.520
walking even like 20 minutes a day,

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:53.920
maintaining a healthy
weight, and not smoking

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:56.640
results in a substantial
reduction in mortality

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.200
even in the following few years.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.680
We’re talking a 40 percent
lower risk of dying

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:03.280
in the subsequent four years.

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:06.320
So, making the necessary
changes to adhere to a healthy

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:09.760
lifestyle could be considered
extremely worthwhile,

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:12.800
and middle age is certainly
not too late to act.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:17.360
As an aside, realizing the
12 to 14 years of added life

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.320
study was based on data
from health professionals,

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:23.360
that got me excited about
all the potential knock-on effects.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:25.520
If health professionals
start getting healthier,

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:28.720
they could become role models
for more healthful living

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.880
and potentially save more
lives than just their own.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:34.000
But that may have just
been wishful thinking.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:38.480
Practicing what you preach
can sometimes backfire.

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:44.240
Evidently, “displays of excellence
can paradoxically turn off

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:46.880
the very people they
are trying to inspire.”

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:50.160
You’d assume that not
being a hypocrite and trying

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:53.200
to walk the walk would just
lead to positive consequences,

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:54.800
inspiring confidence in others, right?

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:59.360
Don’t you want a dance instructor
who can dance, a music teacher

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:02.320
who can play, a health
professional who’s healthy?

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:06.400
But this simple intuition
fails to take into account

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:09.840
concerns about making
other people feel inadequate.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:13.360
Like you know how vegetarians
are the targets of a

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:15.840
surprising amount of
hostility and ridicule.

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.800
That’s because they may
come off as morally superior

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:21.600
and make people feel like
they’re being looked down upon.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.000
There was an elegant
demonstration of this phenomenon

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:27.040
in this study where
“Principled deviants who take

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:29.840
the high road threaten
others’ moral self-worth.”

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:33.280
This is what they did.
Research subjects were asked

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:36.640
to complete a racist
task by an experimenter,

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:40.960
and those moral rebels who
refused to obey were cheered

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:45.760
by observers but were
disparaged by participants

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:48.640
who had themselves
gone through with it,

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:52.720
and for whom the rebel’s stance
thus implied an indictment

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:55.200
of their spinelessness.
Isn’t that interesting?

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:59.040
So, when doctors portray themselves
as “the picture of health,”

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:02.000
patients might think they’re
being holier-than-thou and

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:05.040
unintentionally alienate people,
potentially turning off

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:08.160
the very patients who
most need their help.

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:10.960
You can see how someone with
a weight issue might feel

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:13.840
threatened and judged
by a physician triathlete.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:16.160
But what are we supposed to do?

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:18.080
We want healthy practitioners.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:20.560
Physicians who
smoke are less likely

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:21.840
to tell their patients
to quit smoking,

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:24.080
physicians who are overweight

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:26.400
are less likely to counsel
their patients about weight,

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.000
and physicians
who don’t work out

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.320
are less likely to
talk about exercise.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:34.480
What we can do to make
patients more comfortable

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:37.600
is emphasize that our
role is to help people

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:42.240
meet their own personal health
goals, whatever they may be.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:46.000
Studies show that when doctors
do this, take this approach,

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:49.520
it increases the appeal of
fitness-focused physicians

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:51.120
to overweight patients.

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.520
So, we can then display
exemplary behavior

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:56.960
while at the same time
not inadvertently alienate

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:00.240
the very people who could
benefit most from our guidance.

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:05.200
Finally today, we look at
cancer-causing contaminants

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.567
in medication and meat.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.600
In 2018, one of the bestselling
blood pressure drugs,

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:16.000
valsartan—sold as Diovan—was
found to be contaminated

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:19.920
by the “probably carcinogenic”
nitrosamine known as

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:22.640
N-nitrosodimethylamine (NDMA).

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:26.400
It’s believed that approximately
20 million people worldwide

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:29.760
were prescribed the drug
tainted with this contaminant

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:33.520
whose cancer risk has been shown
to exceed that of many known

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:39.440
potent carcinogens, including
asbestos, benzo[a]pyrene, and PCBs.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:43.120
The U.S. Food and Drug Administration
(FDA) estimated that

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:46.720
taking the drug for a few years
could cause cancer in as many as

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:51.440
1 in 8,000 people, whereas the
European equivalent of the FDA

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:54.880
estimated the cancer risk
could be as high as 1 in 5,000.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:00.000
It is unlikely, researchers
wrote in this Spring 2019 paper,

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.920
that drugs like valsartan
are a unique case.

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:07.200
And indeed, a few months
later, the FDA announced

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:10.480
it had found NDMA in ranitidine.

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:13.920
Ranitidine, the acid
reflux drug sold as Zantac,

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:17.680
is one of the most prescribed
drugs on the planet,

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:20.000
in addition to being
sold over the counter.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:23.680
Give people a single tablet
and the amount of NDMA flowing

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:25.040
through their bodies jumps up&nbsp;

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:27.440
more than a hundred-fold.
&nbsp;

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:33.040
Then in 2020, some formulations
of metformin, a popular diabetes drug

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:36.720
sold as Glucophage, were
found to be contaminated.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.400
The finding of NDMA in common
medicines led the FDA to call for

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:44.240
the immediate withdrawal of
all Zantac from store shelves,

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:46.720
yanking the drug from the
market because their testing

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:51.200
showed NDMA levels could in
some circumstances exceed

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:56.240
the acceptable daily intake
limit of 96 nanograms per day.

00:11:56.240 --> 00:12:00.800
It was so bad that the FDA
found levels of this carcinogenic

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:05.600
contaminant NDMA in Zantac similar
to the levels you would expect

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:10.640
to be exposed to if you ate grilled
or smoked meats! Wait, what?

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:15.280
NDMA has not only been found
in contaminating drugs.

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:18.640
It is a known byproduct of
pesticide manufacturing,

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:22.400
leather tanning, and tire plants,
and is found in multiple

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:26.000
foods and beverages, including
processed meat and beer.

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:29.760
Now that we know NDMA can
transfer through the placenta,

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:33.040
this may explain the relationship
between maternal cured meat

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:36.400
consumption during pregnancy and
the risk of childhood brain tumors.

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:40.720
For example, hot dog consumption
during pregnancy may increase

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:43.840
childhood brain tumor
risk by 33 percent

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:47.600
or sausage consumption by 44 percent.

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:50.560
Bacon consumption may increase
childhood brain tumor odds

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:55.520
by 60 or 70 percent.
But it’s not just processed meat.

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:58.720
Researchers have found it
in poultry products as well.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:05.600
A single serving of chicken contains
more than 100 nanograms of NDMA.

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:09.360
Remember how the FDA said
the acceptable daily intake limit

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:11.680
is 96 nanograms per day?

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:14.160
Half of a chicken
breast contains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:15.840
110.
&nbsp;

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:19.600
Now, raw poultry doesn’t have any;
it’s the cooking process.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:23.840
In fact, the dry-heat cooking of
meat, like broiling or grilling,

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:27.280
even creates airborne NDMA,
releasing this very potent

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:29.440
carcinogenic compound into the air.

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:32.160
So, even if you’re only
getting a salad or something in a

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:35.520
charcoal grill restaurant, just
being indoors where meat

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:39.040
is being cooked could pose
a signiﬁcant cancer risk.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:43.280
These nitrosamines are also
found in cigarette smoke,

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:47.040
and pressure was put on the tobacco
industry to try to remove them,

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:51.440
arguing that there is simply no
logical reason why a removable

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:55.520
carcinogen should be allowed
to remain in a consumer product.

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:58.320
That’s the same reason Zantac
was yanked from store shelves.

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:02.160
Okay, so let me get this straight.

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:06.240
One of the best-selling drugs in&nbsp;history
was pulled from the market—

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:09.440
a drug that brought in billions of dollars—

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:13.040
because it contained a probable
carcinogen that exceeded

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:16.000
the acceptable daily limit,
but there may be more

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:19.120
of the contaminant in a
single serving of chicken!

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:21.600
So, my question is:

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:26.449
why aren’t they pulling the
poultry off the shelves as well?

